    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The sire model used in Example 1 is a variation of the gametic model wherein the dam contribution is ignored (and hence incorporated into the error term).",
      "source_page": 770,
      "printed_page": 750,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter26:p014:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        147.0,
        950.0,
        1105.0,
        1032.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        770
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          147.0,
          950.0,
          1105.0,
          1032.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "0679b6e205cd7dc4eb085194593bad9f410e8ec8e91ba328ea3bfb1744373b31"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The residual error in the gametic model, $ e_{ai} + e_i $, contains a genetic component $ e_{ai} $ (the segregation error) in addition to an uncorrelated environmental component $ e_i $. As in the animal model, the $ e_i $ are assumed to be uncorrelated with common variance $ \\sigma_E^2 $ so that their covariance matrix is $ \\sigma_E^2 $ I. For noninbred parents, the variance of the segregation error is $ \\sigma_A^2 / 2 $, while if the parents are inbred,",
      "source_page": 770,
      "printed_page": 750,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter26:p014:b11"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        149.0,
        1017.0,
        1108.0,
        1225.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        770
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          149.0,
          1017.0,
          1108.0,
          1225.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "498615de92e5b58889e8aa2762362b7b032a939746f10c684850ea517815d9a5"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter26_formula048]]",
      "source_page": 770,
      "printed_page": 750,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter26:p014:b12",
        "Genetics_chapter26:p014:b13"
      ],
